#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171783 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "325cfa6e-a3ff-51f3-9667-95e4ad86ac4c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Simple Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "0bf9999b-16dd-567b-9e52-e80a1b0c6a38" :question/math-academy-id "e-8675" :question/problem "Which of the following curves is *not* simple?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041961/assets/cd4a9fa61745ef862c0c5ea759896ec081f1941806e70ee791d3b9b8970c7f21.png)" :question/worked-solution "A simple curve is a curve that doesn’t intersect itself anywhere between its endpoints.\n\nSo, a curve that intersects itself somewhere between its endpoints is not a simple curve. Among the given options, the only that is not a simple curve is the following:\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041961/assets/0fa8f32c9e9e98b668fabd032754bc0d13365c422f73a3b719070cd7492814cb.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct option is diagram I."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171784 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ca16ec8-183d-5e2e-9715-24107f8aa8c5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closed Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "ee0133c9-056e-5595-b407-8f8f00c49823" :question/math-academy-id "e-8676" :question/problem "Which of the following curves is *not* closed?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041961/assets/5ea305bd29a73c56154ff1d2e338214a63c8f70cc237edce25227bcc325f0333.png)" :question/worked-solution "A curve is closed if its terminal point coincides with its initial point. So, a curve whose terminal point does not coincide with its initial point is not closed. \n\nOf the given options, the only one that is not a closed curve is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041961/assets/da0561c80eac9f87194c19c321b052902ef86b4d90437c5725d7729b719407ce.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct option is diagram III."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171785 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337518a9-39a1-57e3-8686-74a135f7c3d7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Oriented Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "1b8cbd6a-e306-5092-a402-6a0da61c5ec8" :question/math-academy-id "e-8677" :question/problem "Which of the following curves is simple, closed, and negatively oriented?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041961/assets/00b8a75f61696680614e39503bed9dc92ad8ad0228d15db9498c3af44e032fae.png)" :question/worked-solution "A simple closed curve is positively oriented if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is negatively oriented. \n\nA curve is positively oriented if it is traversed counterclockwise and is negatively oriented if it is traversed clockwise.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the curves:\n\n- Curve I is not simple.\n\n- Curve II is simple, closed, negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\n- Curve III is a simple and closed curve but it is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\n- Curve IV is neither simple nor closed.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172585 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "325cfa6e-a3ff-51f3-9667-95e4ad86ac4c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Simple Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81f5d450-b473-51dd-857e-eeae402ac3bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3356/Source/Images/q-97914-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171476} {:answer/id #uuid "9db50d92-